gram about  some of the challenges facing  independent progressive media in general  and if you want to support this type of  work and we have believe me we have no  shortage of ideas right we have actually  a very long list of ideas for long-form  pieces if you want to support not only  the regular show but the extra stuff  like what you're about to see become a  member  40% off with the coupon code vote soon  18 become a patron dollar-a-month  and if it's your last dollar don't give  it to us right I don't want your last  monthly dollar Pat he would accept it  but he doesn't want it okay well I'll  speak for him on in that sense but if  you have a dollar a month to contribute  to progressive independent media  consider doing that at patreon.com slash  david pakman show and I know that lots  of people in our audience do and with no  further delay because I think I've gone  on long enough  let's get to a preview of the  pseudo-science miniseries today starting  with the episode on extrasensory  perception our pseudoscience miniseries  now brings us to extrasensory perception  or ESP which is an umbrella term to  describe any kind of acquisition of  information through means other than the  established physical senses seeing  hearing smelling tasting and touching  someone claims to have ESP when they  tell us that they're able to sense  things outside their body through some  means other than these five senses so an  example would be  telepathy sensing another person's  thoughts clairvoyance sensing events in  another location precognition seeing the  future retro cognition seeing things in  the past that you didn't witness or  mediumship which would be communicating  with the dead or knowing things about  the spirits of dead people some people  call SP a sixth sense a psychic is  someone who claims to have one or more  types of ESP and we deal with psychics  in another episode of the mini-series  there's a 2016 report from Ibis world  which says that 85 thousand people in  the United States work as psychics  collectively earning about two billion  dollars a year and there's a 20-17  YouGov poll which says that a fifth of  Americans have consulted a psychic  in their life and that more than a third  of Americans have personally experienced  psychic ability in themselves at least  one time a quarter of Americans also say  that they can supernaturally predict the  future and 38 percent say that they have  sensed the presence of a spirit during  their life without these numbers you  might not realize how relevant this  pseudoscience miniseries is you might  see these videos on different  pseudo-sciences and think who cares only  wacky people believe this stuff but no  the numbers are stunning and many people  have no idea how pseudoscience pervades  our society so in a 2014 study in the  scientific journal PLoS ONE adults were  shown before and after photos of the  same woman for a second and a half  sometimes they were the same photo  sometimes something was changed about  the woman maybe the color of her hair  whether she was wearing glasses maybe  her earrings changed the people being  tested were asked if the pictures were  the same or different and if they  noticed the difference they were asked  what the difference was mostly people  could correctly identify when there was  a change and when there wasn't but  what's interesting about the results was  that people were able to detect the  change even when they weren't able to  cognitively and consciously identify and  express what the change was and when the  participants were informed of the  results the fact that they were able to  detect changes even without being able  to explain what they were some of the  participants believed that they had some  kind of ESP because they intuitively  knew that something about the photo had  changed but they couldn't quite put  their finger on it and they said well  the reason is some kind of extrasensory  perception one of the study's co-authors  said quote people are correct in  believing that they have an ability to  sense changes in their environment even  when they cannot verbally identify what  those changes are it is just that this  ability is not a quasi magical Sixth  Sense it can be explained in terms of  known processes and this is a major  theme in pseudoscience humans want to  explain what they don't understand  often by enemy  necessary you might not be conscious of  what you're perceiving so you might  interpret your correctness as something  supernatural there's a 2008 study at  Harvard which used brain scans to test  whether people claiming to have ESP were  actually acquiring knowledge that  couldn't be explained through some kind  of normal perception before the study  scientists already knew through  neuroscience that the brain responds a  certain way when it sees something  familiar and a different way when it  sees something new before the subjects  were brain scanned they were shown a  bunch of pictures by way of so-called  ESP what I mean by that is the  scientists would display the photos on a  computer screen in another another room  for example or they would show the  photos to the subjects relative or  partner or friend in a different room  they displayed it in a number of other  ways that someone with ESP might claim  could get them to sense the image  without actually seeing it then they put  them in an fMRI brain scanner and showed  them the same pictures mixed with new  pictures that hadn't yet been used not  only could the subjects not identify  which pictures were new with any  statistical significance but their  brains responded the same way to every  photo responding the way a brain would  respond if it had never seen any of the  photos these studies aren't the full  extent of all research into ESP claims  but it's a sample of how we can actually  test some of these claims all the time  people report unexplained knowledge  having a dream where something happens  and the next day it happens in real life  or you were thinking about someone and  the very next moment you get an email  from them a dream or a feeling coming  true can seem or feel too amazing to be  a coincidence but not when you think  about it like a statistician you spend  your entire life constantly thinking  feeling and dreaming and every once in a  while some of those thoughts and  feelings line up with actual events it  would actually be more amazing if it  never happened much of the time it's  your brain putting things together  without you realizing it many  psychological  go into what makes up our intuitions and  the subconscious inferences we make  every day we aren't fully conscious of  all the perceptions we have and were  rarely conscious of what our brain does  with the information  nothing has proven that anything beyond  our five senses along with our conscious  and subconscious minds are formulating  these intuitions and in fact to the  contrary there's a lot of evidence  showing that our normal senses are  actually what inform us when we think  that we're using ESP many people who  call themselves psychics or mediums or  clairvoyance probably do believe that  they have special abilities some of them  are also consciously fraudulently using  cold reading techniques to improve their  predictions analyzing someone based on  what you already know about them whether  it's their age their clothing their body  language or other characteristics  experiments and studies have failed many  times to prove the existence of ESP  which is a major issue  if you're trying to claim that it's real  but that doesn't prove it's not real  which is what gets us to another problem  with ESP and lots of pseudo-sciences  which is that they are unfalsifiable you  can't prove that ESP definitively does  not occur a skeptics this is when our BS  detector should go off right there's no  experiment or observation that could  disprove ESP so by definition the claim  that ESP is real cannot be a scientific  claim evaluated scientifically or  empirically it's effectively a claim  that is of no use to anyone in the real  world  who cares about evidence some who  believe in ESP might say claims about  ESP aren't supposed to be scientific  because it's by its nature supernatural  and therefore it's not even fair to  subject ESP to the type of empirical  scrutiny that one might want this would  be nonsense because ESP supposedly  interacts with the physical world we're  talking about having visions or  perceptions about what's happening in  material reality so it should be easy  to prove the accuracy of ESP if it were  actually accurate and at this point it's  unclear what kind of a conversation we  can even really have about that element  one of the biggest push backs against  ESP is common sense combined with what  we already know scientifically about the  natural world especially about vision  hearing neuroscience psychology physics  how could ESP possibly work it's a  difficult question for believers in ESP  to answer

